1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,200
You are holding a piece of glass and metal in your hand right now.

2
00:00:03,200 --> 00:00:05,761
You can feel its subtle weight against your palm.

3
00:00:05,761 --> 00:00:08,562
You believe that you are paying full attention to these words.

4
00:00:08,562 --> 00:00:12,002
But in reality, your mind is silently fracturing.

5
00:00:12,002 --> 00:00:15,363
Where does your attention actually go when it slips away?

6
00:00:15,363 --> 00:00:27,124
Your brain is a massive energy consumer.

7
00:00:27,124 --> 00:00:29,724
It constantly seeks the most efficient path.

8
00:00:29,724 --> 00:00:32,245
It creates illusions to make you feel safe.

9
00:00:32,245 --> 00:00:35,726
One of the biggest illusions is the ability to multitask.

10
00:00:35,726 --> 00:00:40,487
You believe you can listen to a podcast and answer an email at the same time.

11
00:00:40,487 --> 00:00:44,607
Why do we have so much false confidence in our mental capacities?

12
00:00:44,607 --> 00:00:50,008
Researcher Clifford Nass at Stanford University spent years investigating this exact question.

13
00:00:50,008 --> 00:00:54,129
He designed experiments to test people who claimed to be excellent multitaskers.

14
00:00:54,129 --> 00:00:59,289
The results showed that these individuals were actually terrible at every aspect of it.

15
00:00:59,289 --> 00:01:01,730
They could not filter out irrelevant information.

16
00:01:01,730 --> 00:01:06,371
They also could not switch between tasks smoothly.

17
00:01:06,371 --> 00:01:09,812
Why does your brain willingly ignore its own clear limitations?

18
00:01:09,812 --> 00:01:13,412
The truth is that your brain does not multitask.

19
00:01:13,412 --> 00:01:17,013
It simply switches its attention back and forth at a very high speed.

20
00:01:17,013 --> 00:01:21,294
This process consumes a massive amount of mental energy.

21
00:01:21,294 --> 00:01:23,894
This concept is known as the switch cost.

22
00:01:23,894 --> 00:01:29,535
Every time you glance at a new notification, your brain has to restart its process from scratch.

23
00:01:29,535 --> 00:01:34,016
Could your evening exhaustion simply be the price of this constant switching?

24
00:01:34,016 --> 00:01:37,056
Imagine you are driving down a familiar road.

25
00:01:37,056 --> 00:01:39,017
You are listening to your favorite song.

26
00:01:39,017 --> 00:01:42,818
Your mind drifts to a meeting you had this morning.

27
00:01:42,818 --> 00:01:45,619
Suddenly a car brakes hard right in front of you.

28
00:01:45,619 --> 00:01:48,859
You slam on your brakes and wonder where that car came from.

29
00:01:48,859 --> 00:01:52,180
How can something so large be completely invisible to you?

30
00:01:52,180 --> 00:01:56,301
This phenomenon is called inattentional blindness.

31
00:01:56,301 --> 00:02:01,141
Psychologist Daniel Simons demonstrated this through a classic experiment.

32
00:02:01,141 --> 00:02:05,662
He asked people to count how many times a group of players passed a basketball.

33
00:02:05,662 --> 00:02:09,743
A person in a gorilla suit walked right through the middle of the scene and thumped their chest.

34
00:02:09,743 --> 00:02:13,224
About fifty percent of the viewers completely missed the gorilla.

35
00:02:13,224 --> 00:02:16,504
Do you think you would have spotted it if you were in that experiment?

36
00:02:16,504 --> 00:02:19,745
You might assume that you are more perceptive than other people.

37
00:02:19,745 --> 00:02:23,426
But our biological architecture is largely the same.

38
00:02:23,426 --> 00:02:28,346
Your brain can only process a limited amount of information at any given moment.

39
00:02:28,346 --> 00:02:33,427
It has to discard most of what it sees to keep you from becoming overwhelmed.

40
00:02:33,427 --> 00:02:36,428
We live in a world filled with invisible gorillas.

41
00:02:36,428 --> 00:02:40,629
How many crucial moments have you missed simply because your mind was somewhere else?

42
00:02:40,629 --> 00:02:44,029
Your attention is like a precious and depleting resource.

43
00:02:44,029 --> 00:02:48,750
When you scroll through dozens of short videos every day, you are eroding that resource.

44
00:02:48,750 --> 00:02:54,591
These applications are designed to trigger the reward system in your brain.

45
00:02:54,591 --> 00:02:58,751
They constantly pump dopamine to keep you lingering longer.

46
00:02:58,751 --> 00:03:02,152
How can you possibly reclaim control over your own mind?

47
00:03:02,152 --> 00:03:07,913
A licensed clinical psychologist can provide you with helpful tools to train your attention if you find it too difficult.

48
00:03:07,913 --> 00:03:11,513
However, change often begins with the smallest of realizations.

49
00:03:11,513 --> 00:03:14,794
You do not need to abandon technology or live like a monk.

50
00:03:14,794 --> 00:03:17,395
You just need to notice when you are becoming distracted.

51
00:03:17,395 --> 00:03:22,156
Is it really that difficult to sit perfectly still and do nothing for five minutes?

